## 1. The Core Formulas and Symbolic Syntax
To evaluate system logic, you must translate plain text code into symbolic Boolean algebra notation.
## The Universal Logical Operators

* Negation (NOT): $\neg P$ (In code: !P, ~P) — Flips the truth value.
* Conjunction (AND): $P \land Q$ (In code: P && Q, P & Q) — True only if both are True.
* Disjunction (OR): $P \lor Q$ (In code: P || Q, P | Q) — True if at least one is True.
* Exclusive OR (XOR): $P \oplus Q$ (In code: P ^ Q) — True if the inputs are different.
* Implication (IF... THEN): $P \implies Q$ — Equivalent to $\neg P \lor Q$. It states: "If P is true, Q must be true."
* Biconditional (IF AND ONLY IF): $P \iff Q$ — Both must have the exact same truth value.

## The Golden Equivalence Laws (The Math to Rewrite Input)
When testing an application, firewalls try to block certain patterns. You use these laws to completely rewrite the mathematical representation of your input so that the firewall ignores it, but the backend server executes the exact same logical output.
$$\begin{aligned} \text{De Morgan's First Law:} \quad & \neg(P \land Q) \iff \neg P \lor \neg Q \\ \text{De Morgan's Second Law:} \quad & \neg(P \lor Q) \iff \neg P \land \neg Q \\ \text{Distributive Law:} \quad & P \land (Q \lor R) \iff (P \land Q) \lor (P \land R) \\ \text{Contrapositive Law:} \quad & (P \implies Q) \iff (\neg Q \implies \neg P) \end{aligned}$$ 
