## 2. Step-by-Step System Testing Using Truth Tables
Let's look at a classic developer error found on registration and payment systems.
The Scenario: A developer writes a code block to handle unauthenticated promo codes outside an account. They want to ensure that a user gets a registration bonus only if the code is valid AND the user has not claimed it before.
They write this logic path:

if (!$code_is_expired || $is_first_time_user) {
    grant_bonus();
}

Let’s map this mathematically to check for an architectural logic flaw.

* Let P = code_is_expired
* Let Q = is_first_time_user
* The developer's expression is: $\neg P \lor Q$

## Building the Evaluation Matrix (Truth Table)

| P (Expired) | Q (First Time) | $\neg P$ (Not Expired) | $\neg P \lor Q$ (Grant Bonus State) |
|---|---|---|---|
| True (Yes) | True (Yes) | False | True (Granted) |
| True (Yes) | False (No) | False | False (Denied) |
| False (No) | True (Yes) | True | True (Granted) |
| False (No) | False (No) | True | True (Granted) |

## Analyzing the Mathematical Failure
Look closely at the fourth row of the truth table.

* The code is not expired (P = False).
* The user is not a first-time user (Q = False).
* The output evaluates to True (Granted).

Because the developer used an OR ($\lor$) operator instead of an AND ($\land$) operator, a returning user can sign up with an unexpired code and completely bypass the first-time user verification check. This mathematical modeling technique allows you to find business logic flaws on registration and voucher check systems without running noisy network scanners.
